   "source": [
    "## Key Findings: Phase 2a (77 Districts, 2005–2023)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Finding 1: Net resource change is small in aggregate but negative overall.**\n",
    "Across all 77 districts, the 19-year net average change in GHI is -1.7%.\n",
    "12 districts show net gain. 65 districts show net loss.\n",
    "Range: +2.5% (Jhapa, Eastern Terai) to -4.5% (Rukum East, Mid-West Hills). This endpoint comparison is sensitive to the specific start and end years\n",
    "chosen, a limitation noted elsewhere in this analysis. A zone-level linear\n",
    "regression across the full 2005-2023 period (see Chart 2 trend statistics)\n",
    "finds only 3 of 13 zones show a statistically significant declining trend\n",
    "at p<0.05: Eastern Mountain, Western Hills, and Mid-West Hills. Eastern\n",
    "Mountain's result should be read with less confidence than the other two:\n",
    "it is a single-district zone (Sankhuwasabha alone, n=1), so its regression\n",
    "is a single-site trend rather than a zone-averaged one. Western Hills\n",
    "(n=10) and Mid-West Hills (n=9) are the two multi-district zones where\n",
    "this finding carries full weight. The remaining 10 zones, including\n",
    "Central Hills, do not show a trend distinguishable from year-to-year\n",
    "noise over this period. The endpoint result above should be read alongside this: a real net change exists atthe district level, but it should not be generalized into a \"Nepal-wide\n",
    "declining trend\" claim without that caveat.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Finding 2: The 2015–2021 trough is the dominant feature of the record.**\n",
    "Across Terai and Hill zones, annual GHI remained 8 to 13% below the 2005 baseline for approximately seven consecutive years (2015-2021) before recovering in 2022-2023.\n",
    "This sustained trough, not the net endpoint change, is the primary risk.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Finding 3: Inter-annual variability is moderate but consistent across zones.**\n",
    "District average CoV: 3.0%. Range: 1.9% to 4.0%.\n",
    "Western Hills districts show the highest variability. High Mountain districts\n",
    "show the lowest. CoV results are consistent with Phase 1 findings.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Finding 4: Deeper losses appear in developable zones at full coverage.**\n",
    "Rukum East (Mid-West Hills) records the largest net decline at -4.5%.\n",
    "Unlike the deepest loss in Phase 1 (Dolpa, High Mountain), Rukum East is\n",
    "a terrain-accessible zone where solar development is practically feasible.\n",
    "This finding was not visible in the 33-district Phase 1 sample."
   ]
