   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2e766e09-1b56-4c18-b803-aa17bcf30ae3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Why the base and downside windows needed to be disjoint**\n",
    "\n",
    "The original base case (2019-2023) and downside case (2015-2021) shared three years\n",
    "of overlap. Those three years namely 2019, 2020, and 2021 include the deepest\n",
    "point of the trough, so both windows contained heavy trough influence, and the\n",
    "two averages converged to nearly identical figures regardless of which baseline\n",
    "they were divided against. The gap was 0.3 percentage points either way. Moving\n",
    "the base case to the non-overlapping 2022-2023 recovery period resolved the\n",
    "problem directly. The resulting gap is 2.7 percentage points (base 100.7%,\n",
    "downside 98.0%), wide enough to differentiate the two scenarios meaningfully\n",
    "in the forward-looking resource context.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Resource case basis used in the DSCR model**\n",
    "\n",
    "Scenario A and B do not reuse the base/downside split from the scenario analysis\n",
    "above. They use a static three-case framework: P50 is the full 2005-2023 mean,\n",
    "the downside case is the 2015-2021 trough average, and the worst case is the\n",
    "2020-2021 average. A rolling replay anchored to a 2005-2010 pre-trough baseline\n",
    "was considered and rejected, since no project in this dataset was actually financed\n",
    "on that basis. Nepal's first utility-scale solar projects were commissioned between\n",
    "2020 and 2023, confirmed against the DOED licensing registry, after the trough had\n",
    "already begun to recover. The three-case design is applied consistently across both\n",
    "scenarios for comparability and does not depend on a short arbitrary baseline window."
   ]
  },
