   "source": [
    "### Financial Findings: DSCR Sensitivity Across Tariff and CAPEX\n",
    "Under trough-average resource at Year 10, Scenario A clears the 1.20x covenant in only 6 of 25 tariff-CAPEX combinations tested. At the generation-1 regulated tariff of NPR 7.30/kWh, the covenant boundary sits between NPR 80M/MW (1.235x, OK) and NPR 85M/MW (1.163x, below covenant), confirming the CAPEX bracket finding above is not an artifact of the two endpoints chosen, the full grid shows the same narrow margin. Below roughly NPR 6.90/kWh, no CAPEX level tested clears covenant under trough conditions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Scenario B clears covenant in 20 of 66 combinations, with 15 combinations falling into outright default-risk territory (DSCR below 1.00). At the CAPEX of NPR 75M/MW used in the main Scenario B analysis, notariff within the tested competitive range (NPR 5.00-6.00/kWh) clears the 1.20x covenant under trough resource at Year 10 — even at the top of the tested range (NPR 6.00/kWh), DSCR reaches only 1.187x. Covenant compliance at this CAPEX level would require a tariff above NPR 6.00/kWh, outside the competitive range this notebook tests.  At NPR 60M/MW, by contrast, covenant compliance holds across nearly the full competitive range. The 75M/MW assumption used in Scenario B sits closer to the fragile end of the CAPEX spectrum than the resilient end, which matters if current-cohort CAPEX in Nepal has continued to fall below the 75M/MW figure modelled here. This notebook does not include independently sourced current-cohort CAPEX data below 75M/MW; the 60-70M/MW range in the heatmap above is a sensitivity bound, not a reported benchmark."
   ]
