   "source": [
    "## Limitations of Phase 2\n",
    "\n",
    "This section consolidates the methodological limitations specific to the\n",
    "77-district expansion and the DSCR financial modeling (Phase 2a-2d). \n",
    "\n",
    "**P90 methodology, validated at annual resolution only.** \n",
    "The parametric\n",
    "P90 figures used throughout Phase 2b (exceedance table, zone summary, DSCR\n",
    "resource cases) assume a normal distribution of annual GHI\n",
    "(`mean - 1.282*std`). This was tested against the empirical 10th percentile\n",
    "of each district's observed 19-year annual series. Only 5 of 77 districts\n",
    "show empirical P90 below the parametric figure, with a maximum gap of\n",
    "-0.33% (Salyan); the average gap across all 77 districts is +0.67%, favoring\n",
    "the empirical figure. The normal-distribution assumption is not understating\n",
    "tail risk at annual resolution in this dataset. This check has not been repeated at monsoon-season resolution, where inter-annual CoV runs 10-20% versus the 2-4% annual CoV this comparison is based on. A heavier-tailed monthly distribution would not necessarily be visible in an annual-level check, and the monsoon P90/P50 chart (Chart 5b) should be read with that gap in mind. Full comparison: `nepal_ghi_p90_empirical_vs_parametric_77.csv`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Stress case is a bounding sensitivity, not a forecast.** \n",
    "The Forward\n",
    "Resource Scenarios stress case extrapolates a linear regression fitted to\n",
    "19 years of data, which contains a visible trough-and-recovery cycle, out\n",
    "to 2038. A linear fit through a trough-and-recovery cycle will\n",
    "systematically underestimate GHI in recovery years and overestimate it in\n",
    "trough years; applying that slope forward compounds the bias. The stress\n",
    "case should be read as an illustrative bounding scenario for lender\n",
    "sensitivity analysis, not a resource projection.\n",
    "\n",
    "**DSCR resource basis relies on a system-wide average; this does not hold\n",
    "uniformly across districts.** \n",
    "Scenario A and B both use a single\n",
    "system-wide GHI average as the resource input. The inter-district\n",
    "correlation matrix (average pairwise r = 0.824) supports this as a\n",
    "reasonable basis in general, but several districts, including Humla (0.554), Mugu (0.592), Jumla (0.677), Dolpa (0.680), and Jhapa (0.718), correlate materially less with the national pattern. A project sited in one of these lower-correlation districts is not well represented by the system-wide DSCR basis used here and requires a site-specific resource series before financing decisions are made on it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Annual DSCR does not capture intra-year cash flow timing risk, and this\n",
    "holds even for a tariff that clears covenant annually.** \n",
    "At NPR 5.94/kWh, Scenario B's base tariff, 7 of 12 months fall below the 1.20x covenant at\n",
    "expected (P50) generation alone under a level monthly debt-service\n",
    "schedule with no reserve buffer, and December already reaches default-risk\n",
    "territory (0.997x) with zero resource stress applied. Under P10 stress,\n",
    "9 of 12 months fall below covenant and 5 reach default-risk territory\n",
    "(July weakest at 0.871, Q3 weakest quarter at 0.916). This does\n",
    "not indicate the project would default monthly in practice: a debt\n",
    "service reserve account sized to the Jul-Dec low season would absorb most\n",
    "of this exposure. But it means the annual DSCR figures reported elsewhere\n",
    "in this analysis should not be read as sufficient on their own for\n",
    "structuring reserve requirements. This is a distinct risk dimension from\n",
    "the tariff/CAPEX sensitivity shown in the DSCR heatmaps, not a restatement\n",
    "of it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Bifacial gain sensitivity is screening-level only (modelled DSCR uplift reported in Key Findings above).** \n",
    "The gain range applied is literature-typical and uniform across resource cases, not per-district or per-project modelled. Mugu's beam-fraction figure specifically should be treated as unverified pending independent confirmation, given ERA5's ~25km grid resolution in that terrain (see Bifacial Relevance Screening section above). Final bifacial gain for any specific project should be confirmed through detailed simulation, not this screening estimate.\n",
    "\n",
    "**ENSO attribution is inconclusive, not ruled out (correlation test result reported in Key Findings above).** \n",
    "A non-significant result means this particular test, at this temporal resolution, did not detect a relationship.It does not establish that ENSO plays no role in the 2015-2021 trough. Aerosol loading from the Indo-Gangetic Plain remains the leading open attribution hypothesis,\n",
    "deferred to a planned Phase 3 using NASA MERRA-2 data."
